</table></div><p class="heritage-longread__aside"><em>Side-branches named in the records but off the direct line: Peter&rsquo;s possible twin brother <strong>Patrick Andrew Docherty</strong> (b. ~1801 Ireland, d. 1871); Thomas&rsquo;s siblings <strong>James</strong> (b. 1831&ndash;32, Ayr) and <strong>Margaret Watson née Dougharty</strong> (b. 1838 Ayr, d. Cable, Illinois) &mdash; the doorway to the American branch; the <strong>Mercer County, Illinois Dochertys</strong> (Thomas B., John Kenneth, Jean Alice, James Henderson); Robert&rsquo;s many Bothwell/Hamilton-born siblings; the <strong>Sullivan</strong> half-siblings Augusta brought into the household; Thomas Richard&rsquo;s brother <strong>Abraham Lincoln Docherty</strong> and his Alix-country descendants; and Thomas Richard&rsquo;s own children <strong>Donald Richard</strong> (&ldquo;Uncle Rick&rdquo;), <strong>Roy Thomas</strong> (1951&ndash;1970), and <strong>David Norman Clark</strong> Docherty (and, through David, <strong>Levi Jesse Docherty</strong>). The brothers and sisters of the Alberta generations &mdash; the nearest of them &mdash; are drawn out in the family appendix below.</em></p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-the-brothers-the-sisters-and-the-alix-household"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Appendix: The wider family</span>The brothers, the sisters, and the Alix household</h2><p>For honesty&rsquo;s sake, this story has followed a single thread &mdash; one child in each generation, the one through whom the line came down. But no family is a single thread, and the people in it were never only &ldquo;heirs.&rdquo; They were brothers and sisters in loud, crowded prairie households. Here, at last, the record is warm enough &mdash; living memory, funeral cards, a McIver family bible, and a typed descendant report kept by Thomas&rsquo;s uncle David &mdash; that we can stop naming only the one who carried the line and start naming the nearest of them.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_3853-scaled.jpg' ) ); ?>" srcset="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_3853-1536x2048.jpg' ) ); ?> 1536w, <?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_3853-scaled.jpg' ) ); ?> 1920w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 920px" alt="Thomas Richard Docherty with his sons Rick and Roy, about 1953" loading="lazy" width="1920" height="2560" /><figcaption>Grampa Tom with Uncle Rick and Uncle Roy, about 1953.</figcaption></figure><div class="heritage-longread__gallery"><p class="heritage-longread__gallery-title">The family album &amp; bible</p><div class="heritage-longread__gallery-grid"><figure class="heritage-line__figure"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_3734-scaled.jpg' ) ); ?>" srcset="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_3734-1536x2048.jpg' ) ); ?> 1536w, <?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_3734-scaled.jpg' ) ); ?> 1920w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 920px" alt="Elizabeth Maryanne with her brother David" loading="lazy" width="1920" height="2560" /><figcaption>Mom and Uncle Dave.</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_3732-scaled.jpg' ) ); ?>" srcset="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_3732-1536x2048.jpg' ) ); ?> 1536w, <?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_3732-scaled.jpg' ) ); ?> 1920w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 920px" alt="Uncle Roy and David Docherty" loading="lazy" width="1920" height="2560" /><figcaption>Uncle Roy and David.</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_3738-scaled.jpg' ) ); ?>" srcset="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_3738-1536x2048.jpg' ) ); ?> 1536w, <?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_3738-scaled.jpg' ) ); ?> 1920w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 920px" alt="Thomas Richard “Grampa Tom” Docherty with his owl" loading="lazy" width="1920" height="2560" /><figcaption>Grampa Tom and his owl.</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_3739-scaled.jpg' ) ); ?>" srcset="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_3739-1536x2048.jpg' ) ); ?> 1536w, <?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_3739-scaled.jpg' ) ); ?> 1920w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 920px" alt="Grampa Tom’s owl, a family keepsake" loading="lazy" width="1920" height="2560" /><figcaption>The owl itself.</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--rotated"><span class="heritage-longread__rot heritage-longread__rot--ccw"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_4534.jpg' ) ); ?>" srcset="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_4534-1536x864.jpg' ) ); ?> 1536w, <?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_4534.jpg' ) ); ?> 2200w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 920px" alt="The Christian Herald Holy Bible, Red Letter Edition" loading="lazy" width="2200" height="1238" /></span><figcaption>The family bible &mdash; a Christian Herald &ldquo;Red Letter&rdquo; edition.</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_4543.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="A register page from the family bible" loading="lazy" width="1237" height="2200" /><figcaption>The bible&rsquo;s register &mdash; Kroeger and Sullivan deaths at Coal Valley, Illinois.</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--rotated"><span class="heritage-longread__rot heritage-longread__rot--ccw"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_4538.jpg' ) ); ?>" srcset="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_4538-1536x864.jpg' ) ); ?> 1536w, <?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_4538.jpg' ) ); ?> 2200w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 920px" alt="Another register page from the family bible" loading="lazy" width="2200" height="1238" /></span><figcaption>More of the register, in the family&rsquo;s own hand.</figcaption></figure></div></div><p><strong>Abraham Lincoln &ldquo;Abe&rdquo; Docherty.</strong> Thomas Richard&rsquo;s brother kept to the Alix country his whole life. He married <strong>Marjorie Evelyn Berg</strong> in 1944 and farmed in the parkland around <strong>Alix and Mirror</strong>. Abe died in 1986; Marjorie lived until 2008, and her funeral was held at <strong>Alix United Church</strong> &mdash; the same small prairie town where Robert had set the family down nearly a century before, still holding Dochertys three generations on. Abe and Marjorie&rsquo;s children &mdash; Thomas Richard&rsquo;s nieces and nephews, and Elizabeth Maryanne&rsquo;s first cousins on the Docherty side &mdash; were <strong>Rob, Allan, Brenda, Lorne, Catherine &ldquo;Cathy&rdquo;</strong> (who married Lorne Conklin), <strong>and Velma</strong>, settling in time across Stettler, Erskine, Mirror, Leduc, and into British Columbia. Through Abe, the Docherty name fanned out across central Alberta, where it lives yet.</p><p><strong>Donald Richard &mdash; &ldquo;Uncle Rick.&rdquo;</strong> The eldest, born in 1946. Richard became Rick, and Uncle Rick he has stayed. He married <strong>Elizabeth Ann Yaremichuk</strong> in 1973, at St Mark&rsquo;s Anglican in Edmonton, and the family records carry a later marriage, to a <strong>Carol</strong>, in 1984. He has children of his own &mdash; a small Docherty cousin-branch the documents on hand do not yet name, and that only the living family can fill in.</p><p><strong>Roy Thomas &mdash; the brother who was lost young.</strong> Born in 1951, Roy died on the 30th of August 1970, in Calgary, at only eighteen &mdash; the grief at the center of this generation, a loss his brothers and sister carried for the rest of their lives. The family&rsquo;s own records hold a tender detail: that Roy came to Thomas Richard and Elizabeth Annie as an adopted infant &mdash; chosen, not born, into the family, and loved as fully as any child of the blood. <em>(This rests on a secondary family data sheet and is set down here gently, flagged for the family&rsquo;s own confirmation.)</em></p><p>Of the other two of the four, <strong>David Norman Clark</strong> &mdash; &ldquo;Uncle Dave,&rdquo; who married Laura Elaine Knutson in 1983 and whose son <strong>Levi Jesse Docherty</strong> is one of the few in his generation to carry the surname forward &mdash; went west with the line to British Columbia; and the youngest, <strong>Elizabeth Maryanne</strong>, is the one through whom it all comes down (Chapter Four).</p><div class="heritage-longread__table-wrap"><table class="heritage-longread__table">
