</table></div><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-where-the-docherty-river-joins-the-others"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">The Living Line</span>Where the Docherty river joins the others</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Calgary · Grande Prairie · the confluence</p><p>Every long family story has a moment when it stops being history and becomes the present. For the Dochertys, that moment is now.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>It has always, somehow, come through.</p></aside><section class="heritage-tree" aria-label="The Docherty family descent, nine generations from Inishowen to Grande Prairie"><h3 class="heritage-tree__title">The Docherty descent</h3><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">John Docherty</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Mary</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">c. 1750s · Ireland</span><span class="heritage-tree__children"><strong>John Edward</strong></span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Two names with <strong>no dates at all</strong> &mdash; the speculative floor of the family&rsquo;s memory, from the tree alone</li><li>The surname Ó Dochartaigh reads like a fingerprint: overwhelmingly a clan of <strong>Inishowen, Donegal</strong> &mdash; a strong likelihood, not a proof</li><li>Poor and of the land &mdash; tenants on the rocky, rain-flayed ground of Ireland&rsquo;s far north</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">John Edward Docherty</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Mary McGeoghan</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">Johnstone, Renfrewshire</span><span class="heritage-tree__children"><strong>Peter</strong><span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Patrick Andrew <em>(twins?)</em></span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Born in Ireland, 1780; died Johnstone, 1862 &mdash; <strong>the man who crossed the water</strong></li><li>Part of the pre-Famine Irish crossing into industrial Scotland, in the Protestant stream that could blend into Lowland life</li><li>Mary &mdash; also recorded <strong>Nancy</strong> &mdash; was Renfrewshire-born, 1786, of an Irish-midlands McGeoghan family</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Peter Dougharty</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Mary Catherine</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">Hamilton · Bellshill</span><span class="heritage-tree__children"><strong>Thomas</strong><span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>James<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Margaret</span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>The spelling slides here &mdash; Docherty, <strong>Dougharty</strong>, Doherty &mdash; the clerks wrote what they heard</li><li>Born in Ireland c. 1801; died Hamilton, Lanarkshire, <strong>3 October 1865</strong>, deep in the coal-and-iron belt</li><li>Mary Catherine was <strong>Govan-born</strong>, 1801 &mdash; in this generation the Irish thread runs through the men alone</li><li>His 1865 Hamilton death certificate is the second key record still waiting to be read</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Thomas Dougharty</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Katherine Brown</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">Ayr → Illinois</span><span class="heritage-tree__children">Peter<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Thomas<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>John<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>James<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Catherine<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Margaret<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Jane<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span><strong>Robert</strong></span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>The first of this line <strong>born in Scotland</strong> &mdash; Ayr, c. 1 January 1835</li><li>Married <strong>Katherine Brown</strong> of Faskine, a daughter of the Lanarkshire coalfield</li><li>Took the family across the Atlantic in the 1890s &mdash; to <strong>Mercer County, Illinois</strong>, not Canada</li><li>Died 13 April 1899, buried in Mercer County &mdash; a Lanarkshire collier laid to rest in American soil</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Robert Docherty</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Augusta Catherina Nitz</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">m. Aledo, Illinois 1908</span><span class="heritage-tree__children">Lena<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Catharina Grace<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span><strong>Thomas Richard</strong><span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Abraham Lincoln</span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Born Hamilton <strong>30 May 1872</strong>; walked away from the pits, through Illinois, to the prairie at <strong>Alix, Alberta</strong></li><li>Married the widowed Augusta at Aledo, <strong>11 July 1908</strong> &mdash; she brought five Sullivan children to the blended household</li><li>The family crossed into Canada in <strong>1912</strong> &mdash; the Illinois years and the crossing now FamilySearch-corroborated</li><li>Augusta died at Alix, 1944; Robert in Calgary, <strong>1959</strong>, aged eighty-seven &mdash; the bridge generation</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Thomas Richard Docherty</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Elizabeth Annie McIver</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">Alix, Alberta</span><span class="heritage-tree__children">Donald Richard<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Roy Thomas<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>David Norman Clark<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span><strong>Elizabeth Maryanne</strong></span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Born <strong>16 January 1914</strong> in Alix and died there in 1977 &mdash; the first fully Canadian generation</li><li>A <strong>Golden Gloves boxer</strong> through the Depression &mdash; family memory says he prize-fought his way home from California</li><li>An <strong>anti-aircraft gunner</strong> at Prince Rupert in the Second World War</li><li>Married <strong>Elizabeth Annie McIver</strong> &mdash; the Hebridean McIver, Campbell, and Cameron lines join here</li><li>Roy Thomas was lost young &mdash; Calgary, 1970, at only eighteen</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Elizabeth Maryanne</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">b. 1959</span><span class="heritage-tree__children"><strong>Thomas</strong><span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Christopher<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Jonathan</span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>The youngest of the four &mdash; Docherty through her father, the Hebridean lines through her mother</li><li>A serving life: restaurant floors across Alberta, later an A&amp;W kitchen in Calgary; now she flags the road-construction season</li><li>Got her son his first restaurant job at fourteen &mdash; there&rsquo;s a fair argument the whole cooking life began with her</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Thomas</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Melanie</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">m. 2016</span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Married <strong>2 July 2016</strong> in Calgary &mdash; the city where Robert&rsquo;s road had ended is where theirs began</li><li>The parents of Patience, Daniel, and Faith</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Patience · Daniel · Faith</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">Grande Prairie</span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Born 2013, 2015, and 2017, all in Grande Prairie</li><li>Heirs of all eight family lines &mdash; the confluence this whole site is built around</li></ul></div></div></div></section><p>The Docherty line comes down through Elizabeth Maryanne Docherty to her son, <strong>Thomas Cheesman</strong>, born in Calgary on the 4th of November. In Thomas, the two halves of his own blood meet: the Lakeman line through his father, Martin Gerard Lakeman, and the Docherty line &mdash; together with the McIver, Campbell, and Cameron Hebridean lines carried through his grandmother Elizabeth Annie &mdash; through his mother. Calgary, where his great-grandfather Robert had died, is where they met.</p><p>But Thomas is not the end of the line, and the Dochertys are not a line at all in the old sense &mdash; not a single thread narrowing to one male heir. Thomas and <strong>Melanie Haiste</strong>, who married on the 2nd of July, 2016, in Calgary, and who are the parents of three children, pass everything they carry, jointly, to those three: to <strong>Patience Kristie-Anne Cheesman</strong>, born in Grande Prairie in 2013; to <strong>Daniel Eric Thomas Cheesman</strong>, born in Grande Prairie in 2015; and to <strong>Faith Lana Lyn Cheesman</strong>, born in Grande Prairie in 2017.</p><p>The three of them inherit it together. Not one as &ldquo;the heir&rdquo; and the others as branches off the side &mdash; all three, equally, as a sibling-group into whose keeping the whole of it is handed.</p><p>And what is handed to them is not the Docherty line alone. It is one of several inheritances now gathered in a single household: the Lakeman line, out of the Dutch polders and the Indies, and the Docherty and Hebridean McIver-Campbell-Cameron lines, all reaching the children through their father; and through their mother Melanie, the Haiste line out of the Yorkshire tanneries and the Rycroft line (with Steinke beside it) out of industrial Leeds by way of Hawai&rsquo;i. And there is the Cheesman name itself &mdash; given not by blood but by belonging, the name Thomas took from his step-father and gave to his children. Five inheritances. Two parents. Three children, in a house in Grande Prairie, carrying the whole of it forward.</p><p>None of the people in this story could have imagined them. John and Mary, on the cold edge of Donegal in the 1750s, landless and poor on the rocky ground of Inishowen, could not have imagined a great-great-great-great-great-granddaughter named Faith being born free in a prairie country that did not exist. Robert Docherty, walking away from a Hamilton colliery toward a quarter-section of Alberta sod, was reaching for exactly this and could not have known its faces.</p><p>The Dochertys tholed. They bore the hungry years and the pits and the crossing and the buried children, and did not break under the bearing.</p><p>That word is now theirs. Patience, Daniel, and Faith &mdash; <em>thole</em>, and <em>come through</em> &mdash; along with everything else this family pours into them. It came through Donegal and the crossing of the Irish Sea. It came through the Lanarkshire dark and the long climb out of it. It came through the Atlantic and the Dust Bowl and the long labour of breaking new ground. It has always, somehow, come through.</p><p>It will again.</p><details class="heritage-longread__notes" id="lr-notes">
<summary>Notes on the records</summary>
<div class="heritage-longread__notes-body">
<p>This appendix records what stands on proof and what stands on the family&rsquo;s own tree. The Docherty line is the <strong>thinnest-documented</strong> of the major lines in this family &mdash; not through carelessness but because it was, for most of its length, a poor working family of exactly the kind the record preserved worst. The shape of the story is secure; many individual links are not yet primary-confirmed.</p><h3>Key records still needed</h3><p>In rough order of value:</p><ol>
<li><strong>John Edward Docherty&rsquo;s death certificate</strong> &mdash; Johnstone, Renfrewshire, 1862. Names his parents; the single document most likely to crack the Irish-origin question.</li>
<li><strong>Peter Dougharty&rsquo;s death certificate</strong> &mdash; Hamilton, Lanarkshire, 1865. Names his parents; independently tests the John Edward → Peter link.</li>
<li><strong>Robert Docherty&rsquo;s Alberta homestead file</strong> + the <strong>LAC Western Land Grants</strong> database &mdash; arrival date, exact land, prior residence, naturalisation.</li>
<li><strong>Glasgow / Atlantic passenger lists</strong> (c. 1895&ndash;1910) and the <strong>1901 / 1911 / 1921 Canadian censuses</strong> for Robert.</li>
<li><strong>The Robert&ndash;Augusta marriage record</strong> (Aledo, Illinois, 1908) and Robert&rsquo;s *<em>1959 Calgary death record + </em>Calgary Herald<em> obituary</em>*.</li>
</ol><p>Both Scottish certificates exist in principle at ScotlandsPeople for a per-record fee (death certificates from 1856 on name <em>both parents</em> of the deceased). Until they are read, generations 1&ndash;3 stand as <em>probable / inherited</em>, not <em>verified</em>.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p><strong>On the telling &mdash; two voices.</strong> This story is told in two voices at once. One is warm and certain &mdash; because the <em>shape</em> of it is certain, the shape of the great Irish migration into industrial Scotland and then across the Atlantic to the prairie, a shape this family unmistakably belongs to. The other is careful, and keeps saying <em>probably</em>, and <em>the tree tells us</em>. Both are true at once, and a good family story does not hide one to make the other louder.</p><p><strong>Origins &mdash; a floor, not a fact.</strong> John &amp; Mary appear in the tree as John Edward&rsquo;s parents, undated and undocumented; treat them as the family&rsquo;s best inherited guess, possibly a placeholder &mdash; not a documented couple. The <strong>Donegal / Inishowen origin</strong> is a surname inference (Ó Dochartaigh is overwhelmingly a Donegal clan name) &mdash; strong but unproven, and complicated by the wife&rsquo;s surname <strong>McGeoghan</strong> (an Irish <em>midlands</em> name), so the family may join two corners of Ireland in Scotland. No Irish baptism will be found online: John Edward (1780) and Peter (~1801) predate every digitised Donegal register by thirty to eighty years. Tithe Applotment books (1823&ndash;37) and Griffith&rsquo;s Valuation (Donegal, 1857) are the realistic tools for mapping surviving kin, not for finding these specific men.</p><p><strong>On religion &mdash; a correction.</strong> An earlier draft assumed an <em>Irish Catholic</em> identity (a Mass rock on the Donegal hill, a chapel built of pennies). That was an inference from the Donegal surname, never a documented fact, and has been removed. Family testimony together with a primary artifact &mdash; the Protestant <em>Christian Herald</em> bible Robert&rsquo;s mother gave him on 30 May 1908 &mdash; establish a <strong>Protestant</strong> family from at least Robert&rsquo;s generation. A Protestant family with a Gaelic Donegal surname most plausibly traces to Ulster Protestant stock, though whether the line was <em>always</em> Protestant or descends from Gaelic Catholics who later conformed is unknown. The lesson stands for the whole project: a reasonable hypothesis (Donegal surname ⇒ likely Catholic) was allowed to harden into a theme before evidence confirmed it; the evidence, when it came, said otherwise.</p><p><strong>Spelling.</strong> Docherty, Dougharty, Doherty, Dougherty all appear for the same family &mdash; clerks transcribing an Irish name by ear. Normal for Irish-Scottish families, and not evidence of separate families; but any future search must run all spellings.</p><p><strong>The Illinois waystation &mdash; a migration correction.</strong> The emigration did not run straight from Scotland to Alberta but <strong>Scotland → Mercer County, Illinois → Alberta.</strong> Robert&rsquo;s father <strong>Thomas Dougharty</strong> took the family across the Atlantic in the 1890s and died in Mercer County in 1899; Robert appears in <strong>Pre-emption Township</strong> in the 1900 and 1910 US censuses, married <strong>Augusta Nitz at Aledo on 11 July 1908</strong>, and the blended household immigrated to Canada in <strong>1912</strong> (living around Medicine Hat, Red Deer, Lamerton, and Camrose County before Alix). The &ldquo;Mercer County Dochertys&rdquo; once thought a distant branch &mdash; Thomas B., John Kenneth, Jean Alice Liebendorfer, James Henderson &mdash; were the family&rsquo;s <em>own</em> people. The <em>1882 History of Mercer County</em> has <strong>zero</strong> Docherty hits in any spelling, so the family arrived after 1882 or below the historians&rsquo; notice; US censuses (1880/1900/1910) and Illinois marriage and naturalisation records are the way in.</p><p><strong>The wives were Scottish-born.</strong> Both <strong>Mary (Nancy) McGeoghan</strong> (b. 1786, Renfrewshire) and <strong>Mary Catherine</strong> (b. 8 Nov 1801, Govan, Glasgow) were Scottish-born; the Irish origin runs through the <strong>male line alone</strong> &mdash; John Edward and Peter. An earlier draft wrongly called Mary Catherine &ldquo;Irish-born&rdquo; &mdash; corrected in Chapter One.</p><p><strong>The Nitz / Sullivan thread</strong> is the best-documented early part, because it is American. The Nitz and Kroeger families are well-attested in Rock Island County (German immigrants from West Prussia and Schleswig-Holstein); Augusta&rsquo;s first marriage to <strong>Warren Martin Sullivan</strong> (Moline, 1894) and her Sullivan children are traceable. Loose end: an earlier record puts Warren Sullivan&rsquo;s death in <strong>1910</strong>, which cannot stand against the confirmed <strong>1908</strong> remarriage &mdash; one date is wrong and needs rechecking.</p><p><strong>The &ldquo;twins&rdquo; question.</strong> The tree suggests Peter (b. ~1801) and <strong>Patrick Andrew Docherty</strong> (b. ~1801, d. 1871) were twin brothers &mdash; unconfirmed, resting on the matching birth year alone.</p><p><strong>Where the line becomes solid.</strong> From <strong>Thomas Richard Docherty (b. 1914, Alix)</strong> onward the story is in the recoverable Alberta record and living memory; those generations are <em>verified / living memory</em>, in contrast to the <em>probable / inherited</em> generations above.</p><p><strong>The living generations &mdash; sources, and one tender flag.</strong> The family appendix rests on living memory and on records photographed by Thomas&rsquo;s uncle <strong>David Norman Clark Docherty</strong> in 2026 &mdash; a McIver family bible, a typed <em>&ldquo;Descendants of Neill McIver&rdquo;</em> report, funeral cards, and data sheets from &ldquo;Aunty Eleanor&rdquo; (Eleanor Golden) and &ldquo;Aunt Bessie.&rdquo; Abe Docherty&rsquo;s family is confirmed by the obituary of his wife <strong>Marjorie (Berg) Docherty</strong> (d. 2008). Robert and Augusta&rsquo;s four biological children &mdash; <strong>Lena, Catharina Grace (b. 1909), Thomas Richard (1914), Abraham Lincoln (1917)</strong> &mdash; are confirmed from the tree (settling that Catharina Grace was a Docherty, not one of Augusta&rsquo;s Sullivan children). Left deliberately <em>un</em>-hardened: the note that <strong>Roy Thomas was adopted</strong> as an infant (a data sheet gives a birth name, &ldquo;Roy Murray Baker,&rdquo; and a Calgary adoption) rests on a single secondary source and is flagged for the family to confirm. One date is also still open: <strong>Thomas Cheesman&rsquo;s own birth year</strong> is recorded as both <strong>1980 and 1981</strong> (the Lineage table carries it as &ldquo;1980/81&rdquo;); which is correct has not yet been settled.</p><p><strong>On the McIver / Campbell / Cameron in-marriage.</strong> Elizabeth Annie McIver carries the Hebridean lines (McIver of Lewis, Campbell of South Uist, Cameron of Moray) and their own well-documented history &mdash; including the verified Great War death of <strong>Norman George McIver</strong> (46th Bn CEF, killed 3 June 1917, buried at Villers Station Cemetery, Pas-de-Calais, France). They have their own full telling and are summarised, not retold, here. Two tree-builder errors to avoid: Norman George did <em>not</em> die at &ldquo;Vimy Ridge, Belgium&rdquo; (it was France), and John Alexander McIver did <em>not</em> die at Saltcoats in 1940 (he died in Edmonton in 1970).</p><p><strong>Records update &mdash; since this draft.</strong> Independent records on FamilySearch have corroborated the Illinois years and the 1912 crossing for Robert and Augusta &mdash; including <strong>Catharina Grace&rsquo;s 1909 Illinois birth</strong> &mdash; moving the fifth generation from <em>probable</em> to <em>verified</em>. The two Scottish death certificates (John Edward, Johnstone 1862; Peter, Hamilton 1865) remain the pivotal documents still awaiting retrieval at ScotlandsPeople.</p><p class="heritage-longread__aside"><em>In short: believe the shape of it, and check the stones. The Docherty story is true in its shape &mdash; Donegal to Lanarkshire to Alberta &mdash; but not yet proven in many of its links. The path from &ldquo;probable&rdquo; to &ldquo;verified&rdquo; runs, above all, through two small Scottish death certificates and one Alberta homestead file. They are waiting to be read.</em></p>
</div>
</details>
